  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sustainable Energy for the Humanitarian Hubs in Northeast Nigeria</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The International Organization for Migration (IOM) operates eight humanitarian hubs in the Northeast region of Nigeria, serving as the primary provider of such interagency common services in the humanitarian sector. These hubs have played a vital role in delivering lifesaving assistance to four million people in need in Borno state. They not only improve access to affected populations but also enhance coordination, collaboration, and effectiveness among humanitarian responders. Ensuring uninterrupted electricity supply to these hubs is crucial for their smooth operation. Currently, the hubs rely on diesel generators, leading to significant fuel consumption (690,000 liters per year) and a carbon footprint of 1,867 metric tons of greenhouse gas emissions annually. This accounts for about 4.3 per cent of IOM&apos;s total global emissions in 2021. In this project, IOM is establishing a reliable, cost-effective and sustainable clean source of electricity to seven of the hubs it operates in Nigeria. Transitioning to solar energy will reduce operational costs, improve efficiency, promote environmental sustainability, and ensure uninterrupted delivery of humanitarian interventions in Borno state. Moreover, humanitarian workers benefit from improved working conditions and have access to critical services.</narrative>
  </description>
